.TH 9SHARE.CONF 5 "2026" "9share" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
9share.conf \- what a machine shares and what it shows, for 9share
.SH DESCRIPTION
The configuration of
.BR 9share (8)
is written in obconf, the notation of the OpenBSD daemons' configuration files: keywords and their arguments, braces for a
block, words that may be put in double quotes, and
.B #
for a comment that runs to the end of the line.  There are two files, with the same format.  An
.B export
block belongs in the user's
.I ~/.config/9share/9share.conf
(or
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/9share/9share.conf ),
a
.B mount
block in
.IR /etc/9share/9share.conf .
.PP
.B 9share export
and
.B 9share mount
write the blocks (and
.B unexport
and
.B unmount
take them away) without changing anything else in the file, so comments and the layout the author gave it stay.
The files may be edited by hand; the daemon notices within a few seconds.
.SS export
.nf
export "projects" {
        directory /srv/projects
        host bob.example.org
}
.fi
.TP
.I name
(after the word
.BR export )
Letters, digits and . _ \- (not starting with . _ \-), at most 64.  The receiving machine uses the same name.
.TP
.B directory
The directory to share: an absolute path of letters, digits and . _ + @ \- / (no spaces).  Required.
.TP
.B host
Where to send it, as
.BR ssh (1)
knows it: a name from ~/.ssh/config, or user@host.  ssh must work from this machine to it without a password.  Required.
.SS mount
.nf
mount "projects" {
        user alice
        at /mnt/projects
}
.fi
.TP
.I name
The name the exporter gave the share.
.TP
.B user
The account on this machine that the exporting machine logs in as.  The share arrives in that account's
.IR ~/.9share/ .
Required.
.TP
.B at
The mount point: an absolute path of letters, digits and . _ + @ \- / (no spaces), created if it is missing.  Required.
.TP
.B access
Who on this machine may use the mount: \fBowner\fR (the default) lets only the account named by
.BR user ,
and the kernel refuses everyone else, root included; \fBany\fR lets every user of this machine in, with the exporting user's rights (the files' own
modes still apply, so what group and others may do depends on them).  Opening a mount to everyone is a decision of the machine's administrator, which
is why it is made here, in a file that must be owned by root and not writable by anyone else (see
.BR PERMISSIONS ),
and not by the exporter.  To let only a group in, use
.B any
and put the mount point where only that group can reach it: in a directory whose group is the one you mean and whose mode is 0750, for example
.IR /srv/team/projects .
(The mode and owner of the mount point itself do not matter once something is mounted on it: what you see are the files' own.)
.PP
Both blocks take one more setting:
.TP
.B hold
How many seconds a connection waits for a new carrier (a new ssh connection) after it was lost, before the session is given up (and the
mount removed): 1 to 86400, default 600.  While it waits the mount stays and what the kernel asks waits too.  The exporter and the
receiver each use their own: give both the same.
.PP
A name may be used once in a file, and a mount point once.  A block with a mistake in it is reported as
.IR file:line:\ message
and left out; the other blocks are used.  A mount point that already holds something that is not 9p is left alone.
.PP
The package installs
.I /etc/9share/9share.conf
as a template of comments with no blocks: nothing is shared and nothing is mounted until a block is added.
.B 9share setup
writes the usual blocks after a few questions.
.SH PERMISSIONS
A configuration file says what is shared and who may use it, so a file that someone else could have changed is not acted on.  A file is used
only if all of these hold:
.IP \(bu 3
it is a regular file (a symbolic link is followed, and what it points to is what is checked);
.IP \(bu 3
it is owned by the account that uses it (\fI~/.config/9share/9share.conf\fR) or by root; the system file
\fI/etc/9share/9share.conf\fR must be owned by root;
.IP \(bu 3
it is not writable by group or others (\fBchmod go\-w\fR); being readable by others is fine, it holds no secret (the secrets are in the
.I NAME.secret
files, which must be readable by their owner alone);
.IP \(bu 3
its directory is owned by the same account or by root and is not writable by group or others, or the file could be replaced.
.PP
A file that is refused is treated as if it had no blocks, and the reason is logged: the daemon mounts nothing from the system file, and takes down
what it had mounted from it; the exporter shares nothing from the user's file, and stops what it was sharing.  The commands that edit a file
.RB ( "9share export" ,
.BR unexport ,
.BR mount ,
.BR unmount )
refuse as well, and change nothing, so that a change is not made to a file that is then ignored.
.B "9share status"
warns about a file that is not used.  The files are looked at again every second or so: when the permissions are put right the file is used again,
with no restart.
.SH FILES
The secret that makes a share's link prove itself with SCRAM-SHA-256 is not in the configuration: it is the file
.I ~/.config/9share/NAME.secret
on each machine, made by
.BR "9share pair" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR 9share (8),
.BR 9share\-setup (1)
